viewwall (0.1.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Viewwall is deprecated in favor of viewpie
    (https://github.com/snoack/viewpie), and this is its last release.
    Presenting every viewport in one atomic KMS commit cannot be built in
    Python on top of GStreamer, and current GStreamer has no hardware H.265
    decoding on the Raspberry Pi.
  * Add a compositor output backend, selected with
    drm.output_backend = "compositor". It blends every viewport into one
    frame on a single sink, so the whole wall goes out in one commit and each
    tile runs at its own feed's rate instead of sharing the refresh rate with
    the others. The cost is a CPU blend of the whole frame. kms-planes stays
    the default and is still the zero-copy path.
  * Let a display name its refresh rate, as in mode = "1920x1080@60".
    Previously the driver chose, which on a panel offering 120Hz meant 120Hz.
  * Run on a Raspberry Pi 5. The DRM card is picked by its connectors rather
    than assumed to be card0, since a Pi 5's card0 is the render-only v3d.
  * Build the container image from the Debian package, so the dependencies
    are named in one place. The image previously shipped no H.264 decoder,
    which a Pi 3 hid and which made every feed fail on a Pi 5.
  * Refuse a camera's audio stream in RTSP setup rather than receiving it and
    discarding it, saving two elements per feed.
  * Rebuild the branch behind a viewport that shows only a trickle of the
    frames its feed decodes, including on a rotating viewport. A tile passing
    one frame in ninety previously looked healthy to every check.
  * Log how many buffers a rotating viewport's output queue held at a switch,
    beside the time figure, which cannot tell a full queue from an empty one.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 02:00:00 -0400

viewwall (0.1.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Decide a feed's repair from why it failed rather than from RTP packet
    counts. The previous test sampled counters on a timer and twice read a
    real wedge as an outage in production, escalating nothing while a tile
    stayed dark: a feed that delivers a burst and stops has already stopped
    by the time two samples bracket it. A feed that delivered video, died
    within fifteen seconds and starved the stall watchdog now has its branch
    rebuilt at once, and the wall restarts if that does not clear it. A
    camera that never delivered a frame is still only reconnected, so an
    outage cannot trigger either however long it lasts.
  * Rebuild the branch behind a viewport that stops showing video while its
    feed keeps delivering. Every other failure signal sits inside the feed
    bin, upstream of the branch, so this failure previously blacked a tile
    indefinitely with nothing logged.
  * Restart a feed that connects but never links video. After a switch was
    power cycled, an NVR closed its side of a connection and left the socket
    in CLOSE-WAIT; rtspsrc read nothing, never reached its own timeout, and
    the feed sat silent for twenty-five minutes with no watchdog, no retry
    and no escalation. Each generation now gets thirty seconds to deliver a
    video pad before it is replaced.
  * Set the log level in the configuration file with "log_level", so raising
    it no longer needs a systemd drop-in. The command line still wins.
  * Log how long a rotating viewport takes to change feeds, and how much
    video its output queue still held at the switch.
  * Share the DRM descriptor between KMS sinks rather than duplicating it.
    Every sink replacement leaked one, and a sink is replaced on every feed
    outage.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 13:30:00 -0400

viewwall (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Restart the wall when a feed stays wedged. A feed that connects, reports
    healthy and dies within seconds survives the branch rebuild viewwall
    already had for it; that case now fails the whole wall so the service
    manager gives it a clean process, instead of leaving one viewport black
    indefinitely. A camera that is merely off cannot trigger this: only a
    feed that reached healthy counts towards it.
  * Never give up on that restart, and spread repeated ones from five seconds
    out to a minute (systemd 254 or newer; older releases retry at a flat five
    seconds). A wall that keeps restarting still shows every healthy camera in
    between, where a stopped one shows nothing.
  * Reload the systemd unit on upgrade. The generated postinst appended its
    daemon-reload after the script's "exit 0", so it never ran and an upgrade
    kept the previously installed unit file. Upgrades from 0.1.2 and earlier
    need one "systemctl daemon-reload" by hand to pick this release up.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 19:09:56 -0400

viewwall (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Paint under the viewports so the framebuffer console no longer shows
    through the gaps, the outer margin, or a viewport whose feeds are all
    down. Configured with drm.background, which takes a hex colour or "none".
  * Add displays.<name>.mode, driving an output at a chosen resolution and
    letting the screen scale it back. Worth setting when the grid does not
    divide the native mode into tiles the size the cameras send.
  * Stop requiring displays.<name>.connector_id when only one display is
    configured, since there is nothing to disambiguate.
  * Report what each viewport actually presents. The metrics line gains
    presented_fps and sink_dropped, which count frames reaching the KMS plane
    rather than frames leaving the queue: on a nine viewport wall those differ
    by four to one.
  * Rename the queue side rate to queued_fps. The journal field VW_FPS is
    gone, replaced by VW_QUEUED_FPS and VW_PRESENTED_FPS; a saved journalctl
    query naming the old field needs updating.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:55:56 -0400

viewwall (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Recover a feed whose branch wedges after repeated restarts, instead of
    leaving its viewport dark until viewwall is restarted by hand.
  * Report a stall watchdog whose timeout matches the startup default, which
    previously logged nothing and looked like a feed the scaling had missed.

